
#import "../../../_lib/kodama.typ": *

#show: kodama

#metadata((
  title: "Almost mathematics",
))

Let $(K, K^(circle), K^(circle circle), pi.alt)$ be a perfectoid field. Then $K^(circle circle)$ is a filtered union of free $K^(circle)$-modules, so $K^(circle circle)$ is flat over $K^(circle)$. Note also that $K^(circle circle)^2 = K^(circle circle) times.o_(K^(circle)) K^(circle circle) = K^(circle circle)$ is an idempotent.

#subtree(
  title: "almost zero",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  A $K^circle$-module $M$ is almost zero if $K^(circle circle) M = K^(circle circle) times.o_(K^(circle)) M = 0$. Almost zero modules form a Serre thick subcategory of the category of $K^circle$-modules.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  We define the category of almost $K^circle$-modules as the quotient of $K^circle$-modules by the Serre thick subcategory of almost zero modules.
  The quotient functor:
  $
    (-)^a : K^(circle)"-"serif("Mod") -> K^(circle a)"-"serif("Mod")
  $
  is a localization functor as well as a colocalization functor, which admits a fully faithful right adjoint:
  $
    M^a mapsto hom_(K^(circle))(K^(circle circle), M) : K^(circle a)"-"serif("Mod") -> K^(circle)"-"serif("Mod")
  $
  and a fully faithful left adjoint:
  $
    M^a mapsto K^(circle circle) times.o_(K^(circle)) M : K^(circle a)"-"serif("Mod") -> K^(circle)"-"serif("Mod")
  $
  The category $K^(circle a)"-"serif("Mod")$ admits a closed symmetric monoidal structure:
  $
    M^a times.o N^a & := (M times.o_(K^(circle)) N)^a \
    hom(M^a, N^a) & := (hom(M, N))^a
  $
  The right and left adjoints are symmetric monoidal, due to isomorphisms:
  $
    hom_(K^(circle))(K^(circle circle), M times.o_(K^(circle)) N) & = hom_(K^(circle))(K^(circle circle), M) times.o_(K^(circle)) hom_(K^(circle))(K^(circle circle), N) \
    & = hom_(K^(circle))(K^(circle circle), M) times.o_(K^(circle)) N \
    K^(circle circle) times.o_(K^(circle)) M times.o_(K^(circle)) N & = (K^(circle circle) times.o_(K^(circle)) M) times.o_(K^(circle)) (K^(circle circle) times.o_(K^(circle)) N)
  $
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  - A perfectoid $K$-algebra is a Banach $K$-algebra $A$ such that the subset $R^(circle)$ of power-bounded elements is open and bounded, and the Frobenius map $Phi : R^(circle) slash pi.alt -> R^(circle) slash pi.alt$ is surjective.
  - A perfectoid $K^(circle a)$-algebra is a $pi.alt$-complete flat $K^(circle a)$-algebra $A$ such that the Frobenius map $Phi : A slash pi.alt^(1/p) -> A slash pi.alt$ is an isomorphism.
  - A perfectoid $K^(circle a) slash pi.alt$-algebra is a flat $K^(circle a) slash pi.alt$-algebra $A$ such that the Frobenius map $Phi : A slash pi.alt^(1/p) -> A slash pi.alt$ is an isomorphism.
]


#subtree(
  title: "finite",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  Let $A$ be a $K^(circle)$-algebra and $M$ be an $A$-module. $M$ is called:
  - almost finitely generated if for every $epsilon in K^(circle circle)$, there exists a finitely generated $M_epsilon$ with $f_(epsilon) : M_epsilon -> M$ whose kernel and cokernel are killed by $epsilon$.
  - almost finitely presented if $M_epsilon$ can be taken to be finitely presented.
  - uniformly almost finitely generated if $M_epsilon$ can be taken to be generated by a fixed number of elements.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "theorem"
)[
  Let $A$ be a $K^(circle)$-algebra. Then an $A^a$-module $M$ is flat and almost finitely if and only if $A$ is projective and almost finitely generated as an $A$-module.
]

#subtree(
  title: "étale",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  Let $A$ be a $K^(circle)$-algebra, $B$ be an $A$-algebra and $mu : B times_A B -> B$ be the multiplication map.
  - $B$ is unramified over $A$ if there exists an idempotent $e in (B times_A B)_*$ such that $mu(e) = 1$ and $e ker mu = 0$.
  - $B$ is étale over $A$ if $B$ is unramified and almost finitely presented over $A$.
  - $B$ is finite étale over $A$ if $B$ is unramified and almost finitely presented over $A$.
]
